<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1975</h1><p class="ref-lede">1975 in world history: 47 events, 2 births, 19 deaths, 2 works, 9 states founded and 16 that came to an end. 268 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a><a href="/years/1972">1972</a><a href="/years/1973">1973</a><a href="/years/1974">1974</a><a href="/years/1975" aria-current="page">1975</a><a href="/years/1976">1976</a><a href="/years/1977">1977</a><a href="/years/1978">1978</a><a href="/years/1979">1979</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Bill Gates and Paul Allen found Microsoft</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Gates and Allen formed a partnership in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Albuquerque</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, to sell a BASIC interpreter for the Altair 8800. Their company went on to dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/personal-computer">personal computer</a> software.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-17">17 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Khmer Rouge take Phnom Penh</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> forces entered the Cambodian capital and immediately drove its population into the countryside. Their rule until 1979 led to the deaths of an estimated 1.5 to 2 million people.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-30">30 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Saigon falls to North Vietnamese forces</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Tanks broke through the gates of the presidential palace hours after the last Americans were lifted out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a>. The war ended and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was reunified under communist rule in 1976.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-17">17 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">American and Soviet spacecraft dock in orbit</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> capsule docked with Soyuz 19, and Thomas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stafford">Stafford</a> and Alexei Leonov shook hands through the hatch. The first international crewed space mission was a landmark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> détente.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-5">5 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lynette Fromme tries to shoot President Ford</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Manson Family follower Lynette 'Squeaky' Fromme aimed a pistol at Gerald Ford in Sacramento, but no round was chambered. A second attempt on Ford came 17 days later.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-lakes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The freighter Edmund Fitzgerald sinks in Lake Superior</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The ore carrier SS Edmund Fitzgerald sank in a violent storm on Lake Superior with the loss of all 29 crew. It is the largest ship lost on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-22">22 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Juan Carlos is proclaimed king of Spain</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Two days after Franco's death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-carlos-i">Juan Carlos de Borbón</a> was proclaimed king. Expected to preserve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, he instead guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s transition to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-25">25 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Suriname gains independence from the Netherlands</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The former colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Guiana on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>'s north coast became the independent Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>. Around a third of its population emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-7">7 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Indonesia invades East Timor</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Indonesian forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a> nine days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fretilin">Fretilin</a> declared independence from Portugal. The occupation cost an estimated 100,000 to 180,000 lives before a UN-supervised vote led to independence.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/green-march" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/green-march.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/green-march">Green March</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Green March was a mass demonstration organised by King Hassan II of Morocco in November 1975, in which about 350,000 unarmed Moroccans crossed into Spanish Sahara to press Morocco's claim to the territory.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/alvor-agreement">Alvor Agreement</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Alvor Agreement was signed on 15 January 1975 at Alvor in Portugal by the Portuguese government and the three Angolan liberation movements, the MPLA, FNLA and UNITA.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/helsinki-final-act" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helsinki-final-act.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/helsinki-final-act">Helsinki Final Act</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Helsinki Final Act was signed on 1 August 1975 in Helsinki by 35 states, including the United States, Canada, the Soviet Union and almost all European countries, at the end of the Conference on Security and Co-operation in Europe.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/bananagate">Bananagate</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Bananagate was a bribery scandal of 1975 in which the US company United Brands, formerly the United Fruit Company, was found to have paid 1.25 million dollars to Honduran officials to secure a reduction of an export tax on bananas.</p></div></li></ol></section>
